          A metro that keeps growing sends more calls to every shop on the corridor.
          The desk does not grow at the same rate. The owner is still the person
          who was supposed to pick up.
        </p>
        <p>
          The miss is usually geography, not demand. Crews spend the day on the
          I-49 corridor, between a job in one city and the next job up the road.
          The phone rings in the truck. Answering means pulling over, or not
          answering. Most of those callers do not wait.
        </p>
        <p>
          Springdale adds a clock to that. B2B vendors around Tyson see calls
          bunch up at shift change. A small office cannot take the cluster. The
          calls that ring out in that window are the ones a text can still catch.
        </p>
        <p>
